  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "amyloid-modeling-e938",
  "summary": "Recent models isolate specific mechanical terms and test them against collective migration, order measures, and organelle-driven jamming.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is explicit parameterization of a specific mechanical or biochemical component, with its consequences tested at the level of cell or tissue behavior. A standardized nematic-order framework applied to twelve cell types reports that topological defects show subdiffusion, contrary to active nematic theory. An agent-based model couples mitochondrial motility, morphology and fission-fusion to a deformable axon, with jamming severity depending on shape and stiffness. A phase-field model of Drosophila border cell cluster migration introduces a Tangential Interface Migration force driven by nurse cell contact. A mean-field model couples Rho GTPase reaction-diffusion with cytosolic and cortical Marangoni flows to reproduce diverse cell shapes and locomotion modes. The new element is direct quantification of individual force terms and order measures against emergent behavior, although these studies differ considerably in scope and system.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-28"
}
